From 163aee96a51ed85a10a936350084194745182531 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yang Tsao <yang@fydeos.io>
Date: Mon, 18 May 2026 17:54:34 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 25/75] WM: keep live task bounds across in-task relaunch in
 container mode
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

On fydroid-rt, when the user snaps Settings to half-screen and then
clicks the Chrome maximize button, AOSP relaunches the resizable
activity for the snap → fullscreen-equivalent bounds change. After the
relaunch, opening any nested fragment (Settings → SubSettings) calls
Task.addChild → ActivityRecord.onParentChanged →
Task.updateOverrideConfigurationFromLaunchBounds → getLaunchBounds(),
which used to return mLastNonFullscreenBounds. That field is
intentionally frozen at the last *pre-fullscreen* freeform value
(the strict-less-than guard in Task.onConfigurationChanged skips
display-sized bounds), so getLaunchBounds was handing back the stale
snap-half rect and AOSP shrank the live 2560×1504 task back to
1238×1440. The SF buffer then renders at the small size while Chrome
still shows the maximized frame, so the user sees a stretched window.

Fix in two places:

* Task.getLaunchBounds() — for freeform standard activities in
  container builds, prefer the task's live bounds over
  mLastNonFullscreenBounds when adding a child activity into an
  existing task. The frozen snap rect is the wrong source of truth
  here; the task's current bounds reflect what the user / Chrome
  actually wants.

* LaunchParamsPersister.saveTaskToLaunchParam — likewise persist
  task.getBounds() rather than mLastNonFullscreenBounds, so a
  cross-session restore picks up the actual last size (including a
  maximized one) instead of the pre-maximize freeform value.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Change-Id: Id696a784e2ce657400febc7d5136eeadc9d1db10
---
 .../server/wm/LaunchParamsPersister.java      | 20 ++++++++++++++++++-
 .../core/java/com/android/server/wm/Task.java | 17 ++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 36 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/services/core/java/com/android/server/wm/LaunchParamsPersister.java b/services/core/java/com/android/server/wm/LaunchParamsPersister.java
index b0f3fa28af15..77343d33633c 100644
--- a/services/core/java/com/android/server/wm/LaunchParamsPersister.java
+++ b/services/core/java/com/android/server/wm/LaunchParamsPersister.java
@@ -274,7 +274,25 @@ class LaunchParamsPersister {
         changed |= params.mWindowingMode != wm;
         params.mWindowingMode = wm;
 
-        if (task.mLastNonFullscreenBounds != null) {
+        // fydroid-rt: persist the task's *current* bounds rather than the
+        // pre-fullscreen freeform snapshot. AOSP's stock behavior keeps
+        // mLastNonFullscreenBounds frozen at the last freeform value when
+        // the user maximizes (the strict-less-than guard in
+        // Task.onConfigurationChanged skips display-sized bounds), so a
+        // close-and-reopen of a maximized window would launch back at the
+        // pre-maximize size. More importantly, when AOSP relaunches a
+        // resizable activity on a snap→maximize resize, the relaunched
+        // instance reads back from this persister, picks up the stale
+        // snap-half bounds (e.g. 872×1440 on a 2560×1600 display) and the
+        // Settings buffer ends up stretched inside Chrome's maximized
+        // frame. Use task.getBounds() so the relaunched task starts at
+        // whatever size the user actually has the window at right now.
+        final Rect liveTaskBounds = task.getBounds();
+        if (android.os.Build.IS_CONTAINER && liveTaskBounds != null
+                && !liveTaskBounds.isEmpty()) {
+            changed |= !Objects.equals(params.mBounds, liveTaskBounds);
+            params.mBounds.set(liveTaskBounds);
+        } else if (task.mLastNonFullscreenBounds != null) {
             changed |= !Objects.equals(params.mBounds, task.mLastNonFullscreenBounds);
             params.mBounds.set(task.mLastNonFullscreenBounds);
         } else {
diff --git a/services/core/java/com/android/server/wm/Task.java b/services/core/java/com/android/server/wm/Task.java
index 9f96ae403bd2..e4d8aa50e5a8 100644
--- a/services/core/java/com/android/server/wm/Task.java
+++ b/services/core/java/com/android/server/wm/Task.java
@@ -2349,6 +2349,23 @@ class Task extends TaskFragment {
         } else if (!getWindowConfiguration().persistTaskBounds()) {
             return rootTask.getRequestedOverrideBounds();
         }
+        // fydroid-rt: prefer the task's *live* bounds over mLastNonFullscreenBounds
+        // when launching a child activity into an existing task.
+        // mLastNonFullscreenBounds is intentionally frozen at the last
+        // pre-fullscreen freeform value (the guard in onConfigurationChanged
+        // skips display-sized bounds, so a snap→maximize transition leaves
+        // mLastNonFullscreenBounds holding the snap rect). When a new
+        // activity (e.g. Settings → SubSettings) is added to a task that
+        // is currently maximized via Chrome, getLaunchBounds() must NOT
+        // pull the task back to the stale snap rect, otherwise the SF
+        // buffer shrinks to that size while Chrome still displays the
+        // maximized frame, and the user sees a stretched window.
+        if (android.os.Build.IS_CONTAINER) {
+            final Rect liveBounds = getBounds();
+            if (liveBounds != null && !liveBounds.isEmpty()) {
+                return liveBounds;
+            }
+        }
         return mLastNonFullscreenBounds;
     }
 
